        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: We'll hear argument next in No. 01-301, Tom L. Carey v. Tony Eugene Saffold. Mr. Cross."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Mr. Cross, would you explain to us what it is in California -- what is the system and the time within which post-conviction relief can be applied for in California courts? Is there any time limit for filing it with the trial court level?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Now, after the trial court resolved it and denied relief, then there's no review mechanism other than filing an original writ with the court of appeals?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: So, you -- you can begin in a higher court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But almost always they'll go to the superior court and file a petition. And then in every other State, the losing party would appeal, but in California, they use different words. The words are not you appeal; the words are you file a new petition in the court of appeals."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Is that what a majority of courts are holding?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Criminal Justice Legal Foundation",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0001-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One suggestion that was made in the amicus briefs by the Criminal Justice Legal Foundation is to adopt the nearest analogous State rule, which in California under rule 2 or 30 through 31 would be 60 days."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What do we do?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Ogden",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, that the rule adopted by the -- by the unanimous courts of appeals that any gaps between stages of the State habeas process should be tolled so long as the next stage is initiated in a timely fashion should apply to California."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What happens if it -- go on. Give us your second --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Ogden",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Now, California's system of original writ should be subject to the rule that tolls the entire period of time for two reasons. First, that is the best understanding of the text of the statute because California's system --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you have to?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Okay. Then I think perhaps you should cite some authority."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Ogden",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This -- this Court's decision in the O'Sullivan case has been applied by the Ninth Circuit in -- in the case called James against Giles, which states that it is a requirement, in order to fully exhaust one's State remedies, that one file and submit one's claims to the California Supreme Court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Whichever one it is, you have to exhaust those remedies before you apply for Federal habeas."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. OGDEN: That would -- that would undermine\nthe so-called gap theory in every State but -- but\nCalifornia where typically you do have appeals on both\nsides. In California, you have a State appeal and a\nfunctional appeal on the petitioner's side. And so, the\nargument could be made -- I think it's a hyper-technical\nargument. I think it's an argument that's inconsistent\nwith the purpose of this statute, but you could make the\nhyper-technical argument that because California gives\nunequal rights to the two sides, an appeal on the one hand\nand a functional appeal on the other, we're going to treat\nthe functional appeal in a different way for Federal\npurposes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: -- prevail?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CROSS: I think the answer to Mr. Ogden,\nYour Honor, is that the reason would be that Congress was\nconcerned about undue delay. In the last 10 years before\nAEDPA was passed, over 80 bills were attempted to -- to\ndeal with this problem. And lack of diligence by\npetitioners was the main concern of Congress, and they\ndealt with that by writing into the statute the following\nphrase. 2244(d)(2) at page 3 of the blue brief. Quote,\nthe time during which a properly filed application for\nState post-conviction or other collateral review with\nrespect to the pertinent judgment or claim is pending\nshall not be counted toward any limitation under this\nsubsection. They did not choose to use the -- the"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-01-301",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1b0f78afcf23041ee638f1c8e85eb0fd65471d0468ac1cb81087694ce4b4333c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1b0f78afcf23-0003-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
